WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
Data control is the foundation of sovereign AI.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:07.710
An AI workflow may process prompts, uploaded files, retrieved documents, embeddings,

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:12.660
chat history, generated outputs, tool results, logs, telemetry, evaluation records,

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.600
and user feedback.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.030
Some of that data may be public.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:19.635
Some may be internal. Some may be confidential, regulated, customer-specific, personal,

00:00:19.635 --> 00:00:21.005
or operationally sensitive.

00:00:21.005 --> 00:00:24.990
A practical program starts by classifying the data that can enter each workflow.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.300
Teams should know whether prompts may include customer records, source code,

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.790
contract language, incident details, credentials, health information, financial data,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.200
or proprietary strategy.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.114
They should also understand derived data.

00:00:36.114 --> 00:00:38.045
Embeddings, summaries, evaluations,

00:00:38.045 --> 00:00:41.510
and logs can carry sensitive meaning even when they do not look like the original

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:42.035
document.

00:00:42.035 --> 00:00:44.360
Privacy boundaries can be broken accidentally.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.840
Sensitive prompts may be routed to the wrong service.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.440
Logs may be retained too long.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.565
Embeddings may be stored without a clear owner.

00:00:50.565 --> 00:00:53.400
Retrieval systems may ignore document permissions.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.120
Monitoring data may be shared more broadly than the underlying content would allow.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.745
These are design and operations problems, not only legal concerns.

00:01:00.745 --> 00:01:04.240
Sovereign AI needs explicit rules for what data can enter each workflow,

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.440
where it can be stored, who can inspect it, how long it can be retained,

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.200
and when it must be deleted or reviewed.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.790
Separate inference data from retrieval data and operational telemetry.

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.725
Preserve tenant and permission boundaries.

00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:17.960
Use redaction and access control where logging is necessary.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.335
Strong data control makes later decisions about models, deployment,

00:01:21.335 --> 00:01:22.530
and policy much easier.
